  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front moves offshore this evening.\nTurning noticeable colder Tuesday into Wednesday.\n\nA cold front across western New England as of this writing will\nfinally move offshore later this evening. There is a risk for a\nbrief sprinkle or shower as this front moves by, but the main\nimpact will be clouds and a shifting gusty wind. Wind gusts\ndiminish after sunset, then increase once again during the\ndaylight hours Tuesday.\n\nA large high pressure centered southwest of the Great Lakes \nwill then take control of our weather through Wednesday night. \nThe position of this high pressure will usher colder air into \nsouthern New England. Expecting high temperatures to be 15-20 \ndegrees lower Tuesday, which would still only be slightly below \nnormal.\n\nThe colder conditions will become more entrenched Tuesday night.\nLight north winds and a mainly clear sky will permit low\ntemperatures in the teens and lower 20s away from the immediate\ncoastline. Sunshine Wednesday will provide some recovery, but\ntemperatures should remain below normal for late November\nthrough Wednesday night.\n\nDry weather prevails Tuesday through Wednesday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal storm will bring heavy rain, strong winds, \ndangerous marine conditions, and coastal flooding/beach erosion to \nsouthern New England Thanksgiving through Friday. \n\nHigh confidence in a strong storm impacting the latter half of the \nThanksgiving holiday into Friday. In the upper levels a piece of \nnorthern stream energy will phase with southern stream energy and \ninduce a deepening surface low. This low makes its way up the east \ncoast, running into the existing Canadian maritime high pressure \nthen passing over Nova Scotia by late Friday/Saturday. The \ntightening pressure gradient will result in strong E winds at the \nsurface and an anomalously strong LLJ (3-4 standard deviations above \nnormal). This will result in a prolonged period of gusty east winds \nlate Thursday/Thursday night, as strong as 40 to 55 mph widespread, \nwith bufkit soundings suggesting potential for 60 mph gusts on the \nCape and islands. Winds will remain breezy on Friday but the \nstrongest gusts will be associated with the core of the LLJ \novernight. \n\nThis timing will have a big impact on the coastal flood threat. As \nthe previous forecaster mentioned, minor coastal flooding and beach \nerosion is likely given the strength and duration of the winds \ncombined with astronomically high (essentially maxed out) tide \ncycles on Thursday and Friday afternoons. The good news at the \nmoment is that the strongest winds and greatest surge of 1-1.5 ft \nright now occurs overnight, between these higher high tides. This \nwould help spare SNE from the worst of the coastal flood threat, \nassuming the timing doesn't shift a few hours in either direction. \nAt this point, 3+ days out, exact timing and track of the storms is \nby no means set in stone, so peak winds and high tide could still \nend up coinciding. continue to check back as we get closer. When all \nis said and done, we expect 1-2 inches of rain from this system \n(with locally higher amounts) and thus aren't expecting any \nwidespread flooding from rainfall. \n\nAs the storm lifts out on Friday drier air is pulled in shutting off \nthe widespread rain. The upper trough lingers overhead, though, \ncombined with some lingering moisture at 850 mb so scattered diurnal \nshowers are likely. By next weekend weak ridging looks to bring \nquieter weather but discrepencies amongst the guidance as to how \npersistent this ridge is leads to a low confidence forecast.",
